    "chapter_label": "Why Earth supports life",
    "subtopic_id": "science/grade_08/ch_13/H012",
    "stem": "Siddharth learns that in sexual reproduction each gamete carries half of a parent's genetic material. A male gamete and a female gamete then join at fertilisation.",
    "item_type": "structured",
    "options": {},
    "statements": [],
    "parts": [
      {
        "id": "i",
        "text": "Which claim correctly describes what this arrangement does?",
        "marks": 1,
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "Half-material gametes prevent genetic instructions from doubling each generation, while fertilisation still mixes information from two parents."
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "Sexual reproduction is the same as asexual copying, so gametes never carry half the material."
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "Fertilisation still mixes two parents even if each gamete carried a full set, and doubling never matters."
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "Fertilisation comes first, and only afterward do parents form half-material gametes."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ii",
        "text": "Give one reason for your choice.",
        "marks": 1
      }
